# What does not go into a container build context.
#
# **The build context is the repository root**, so every file here would be sent
# to the builder and copied into the image layer unless this file says otherwise --
# and `mock-cdn.Containerfile` and `mock-foreign.Containerfile` both end their
# build stage with `COPY . .`, which means "the whole context". This file is what
# makes that line mean "the whole *checkout*".
#
# The first version of the foreign mock's Containerfile had no such file, and the
# build failed on a full disk rather than on anything to do with the resolver:
#
# ```text
# Error: committing container for step ... Command:copy Args:[. .]:
#   writing blob: storing blob to file "/var/tmp/container_images_storage…/1":
#   no space left on device
# ```
#
# The context was 850 MB of which 850 MB was `build/` and a reviewer's Go cache --
# neither of which any image reads. So the failure looked like a disk problem on
# the operator's machine and the fix was a file, which is the shape of failure this
# whole harness has been written against.
#
# Each entry says why, because a line here that nobody can justify is a line a
# later reader will delete to make a build work -- and deleting it will put a
# gigabyte back into every context.
#
# The exclusion is by path, and the paths are the ones the build genuinely does
# not read. **The Go module's own sources are NOT excluded**: `COPY . .` is what
# puts `tests/podman/mock-cdn` and `tests/podman/mock-foreign` into the build, and
# a context that dropped them would fail at `go build` with a message about a
# missing package rather than about the image.

# Build outputs. `make package`, `make build` and every image build write here,
# and the .deb alone is 16 MB. Nothing in the repository is *read* out of `build/`
# by a Containerfile -- the images copy `configs/`, `packaging/` and
# `tests/podman/`, and the .deb is built by the host, not inside a container.
build/

# A reviewer's Go build cache, which is 573 MB on this host and is nobody's source.
# It is untracked, so nothing depends on it being in the context, and an
# untracked directory is exactly the kind of thing that makes a build's size
# depend on what someone happened to leave lying around.
.gocache-review/

# The worktree's own git file -- this is a linked worktree, so `.git` is a file
# naming the real gitdir rather than a directory of objects -- and the gitdir
# itself is outside the context anyway. A full `.git` is tens of megabytes and no
# image reads it.
.git

# Python bytecode, which the local suites write and which no image needs.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# The review scratch directory, for the same reason as the Go cache.
.tmp-review/
